{
  "abstract": "Yolcu, Aaronson and Heule asked which residue sets $A$ mod $2^m$ must be met by every nonconvergent Collatz trajectory; they proved the mod-4 cases and $\\{2\\},\\{3\\},\\{4\\},\\{6\\},\\{1,5\\},\\{1,7\\}$ mod 8 and left $\\{1\\},\\{5\\},\\{7\\},\\{5,7\\}$ mod 8 open. We study the certificate $\\log_2 x+\\Phi(x \\bmod 2^g)$ with negative drift on the non-simple strongly connected components of a residue graph, plus exclusion of periodic patterns through their rational pins. This is a special case of Nashida's staged valuation certificates, and the drift condition is his disentangledness; Monks, Monks, Monks and Monks earlier certified and tabulated forward and strongly sufficient residue sets by related cycle criteria. Theorem A states it with its finite hypothesis (all $n<X_0$ converge) and exact arithmetic. Theorem B gives a new equivalent form of the drift condition: every ergodic invariant measure of nonnegative drift on the avoiding 2-adic set has entropy zero. Each open class carries such measures of positive entropy ($h_+=.1967$ per $C$-step for $\\{5,7\\}$, in closed form). Exact computations cover all sets mod 8 and $|A|\\le 3$ mod 16 and mod 32. A closure test reduces the statements not derivable from known results to a basis: two pairs mod 16 (whose divergence half is in the tables of Monks et al.; the cycle exclusion is new), one pair and nine triples mod 32, and one cascade statement at modulus $2^{13}$. Finally, every computed failure is caused by a negative rational periodic point, and near the cycle of $-5$ explicit homoclinic words give failures at every 2-adic depth: avoidance of $7$ mod 8 together with the $-5$ cycle is certified up to $g=13$ and fails for all $g\\ge 14$. This is an explicit mechanism for one failure; that such certificates settle no open class at all was proved earlier by Nashida. No open class is resolved.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "Version 3 adds the prior work that versions 1 and 2 omitted. Monks, Monks, Monks and Monks (Discrete Math. 2013) and Nashida (Parts I and II, Zenodo 2026) are cited and compared in Sections 1, 2 and 7. Theorem A, condition (P) and Corollary C are credited to Nashida in substance; Theorem B's entropy form, the closure bases and the negative-pin cascade are stated as this paper's contributions. For the mod-16 basis {9,15},{11,15}, the divergence half is credited to Monks et al. Table 2. The Section 6 barrier is described as weaker than Nashida's Part II Proposition 8.1 with Part I Theorem 5.2. A new supplement script, closure_nashida82.py, checks that no basis set follows from Nashida's Theorem 8.2(a),(b) by the closure test, with controls. No result was changed or withdrawn.",
